Gives keyboard focus to a mapped layer surface as it asks for on-demand
interactivity, as a newly mapped on-demand surface already gets it, and
gives it back to the one that asked before when the focused one lets go.

A shell keeps its launcher mapped while closed, so it opens at once, and
asks for the keys only as it opens. niri gave on-demand focus only on a
first map or a click, so such a launcher had to ask for exclusive focus,
and an exclusive surface beats every window: a click on one left the
keys in the launcher. Between two exclusive surfaces on a layer niri
takes the first found, not the newest, so notices held over the open
launcher never had the keys.

Now the newest to ask, by its interactivity or a click, has focus, and
the newest still asking has it back when that one lets go. Only a
pointer giving a window focus (a click, a drop, a double-click resize)
forgets who asked, so a surface closing later never takes focus back
from the window. An action, as a bind or a client's IPC runs, and a
window opening on its own leave focus with the surface that asked, as
exclusive focus did, so a shell moving windows from its open launcher
keeps the launcher's keys.

It sets NIRI_ON_DEMAND_WHEN_ASKED=1 for its children and imports it
into the session, so a shell restarted by a switch, under a niri still
running without this patch, keeps asking exclusively until niri
restarts.

